1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,620
Okay, the next section deals with pulmonary hypertension.

2
00:00:03,160 --> 00:00:06,000
Recall that we have two independent circuits coming off the heart.

3
00:00:06,100 --> 00:00:08,720
We've got a systemic circuit and we've got a pulmonary circuit.

4
00:00:09,140 --> 00:00:11,620
Of course, the systemic circuit can undergo hypertension,

5
00:00:11,800 --> 00:00:16,100
and we've discussed that in extensive detail in the vascular chapter.

6
00:00:16,440 --> 00:00:18,560
And now we're going to focus a little bit, very briefly,

7
00:00:18,700 --> 00:00:22,320
on pulmonary hypertension, or high pressure, within the pulmonary circuit.

8
00:00:22,840 --> 00:00:25,540
Now, normally, the pressure in the pulmonary circuit is fairly low.

9
00:00:25,600 --> 00:00:27,200
It's only about 10 millimeters of mercury.

10
00:00:27,200 --> 00:00:33,600
However, were the pressure to increase, for example, beyond 25 millimeters of mercury,

11
00:00:33,820 --> 00:00:36,600
that would then be defined as pulmonary hypertension.

12
00:00:38,200 --> 00:00:41,780
Pulmonary hypertension is characterized by a few important pathologic changes.

13
00:00:42,220 --> 00:00:44,620
You get atherosclerosis of the pulmonary trunk.

14
00:00:44,920 --> 00:00:50,000
The idea here is that if there's high pressure on a vessel that increases the risk of atherosclerosis,

15
00:00:50,320 --> 00:00:54,500
you're well aware of the fact that hypertension is a risk factor for atherosclerosis.

16
00:00:54,500 --> 00:00:59,840
In addition, you get smooth muscle hypertrophy of the pulmonary arteries with some intimal fibrosis.

17
00:01:00,240 --> 00:01:01,840
These are also classic findings.

18
00:01:02,360 --> 00:01:06,060
And perhaps the most important on this slide are plexiform lesions.

19
00:01:06,320 --> 00:01:09,240
This is what occurs when you have severe, long-standing disease.

20
00:01:09,740 --> 00:01:15,900
And a plexiform lesion refers to a tuft or a group of capillaries that are placed together

21
00:01:15,900 --> 00:01:19,900
that arise as a complication of long-standing pulmonary hypertension.

22
00:01:20,460 --> 00:01:24,940
And I've circled a tuft of capillaries here on your slide.

23
00:01:25,560 --> 00:01:30,220
When patients have pulmonary hypertension, this leads to right ventricular hypertrophy

24
00:01:30,220 --> 00:01:35,220
because obviously the right heart has to pump against that high pressure in the pulmonary circuit,

25
00:01:35,520 --> 00:01:38,500
and eventually the right heart can fail, which is called core pulmonale.

26
00:01:38,500 --> 00:01:44,260
The classic presenting sign in patients with pulmonary hypertension is exertional dyspnea.

27
00:01:44,760 --> 00:01:48,920
And were that to progress, they could eventually present with right-sided heart failure.

28
00:01:49,200 --> 00:01:50,180
But here's the high yield.

29
00:01:50,520 --> 00:01:53,920
If a patient presents with exertional dyspnea, especially a young woman,

30
00:01:54,180 --> 00:01:56,520
you want to think about pulmonary hypertension.

31
00:01:57,200 --> 00:01:59,720
Now that leads us to the etiology of pulmonary hypertension.

32
00:02:00,240 --> 00:02:04,200
And remember that pulmonary hypertension can be divided into primary or secondary.

33
00:02:04,760 --> 00:02:06,460
Primary, the etiology is unknown.

34
00:02:06,460 --> 00:02:09,220
It's classically seen in young adult females.

35
00:02:09,780 --> 00:02:15,600
And there is a familial form that's related to inactivating mutations of BMPR2.

36
00:02:15,740 --> 00:02:16,640
That's high yield.

37
00:02:16,940 --> 00:02:17,840
It comes up on exams.

38
00:02:18,440 --> 00:02:23,020
When this gene is inactivated, you get excess proliferation of vascular smooth muscle.

39
00:02:23,660 --> 00:02:26,820
And, of course, thickening of the blood vessel wall with vascular smooth muscle

40
00:02:26,820 --> 00:02:29,300
would lead to pulmonary hypertension.

41
00:02:29,840 --> 00:02:33,860
So again, high yield, BMPR2 inactivating mutations.

42
00:02:33,860 --> 00:02:37,980
It's also possible to develop secondary pulmonary hypertension.

43
00:02:38,520 --> 00:02:40,140
This could arise with hypoxemia.

44
00:02:40,500 --> 00:02:46,740
You're well aware of the fact that the vessels in the lung, they constrict when there is hypoxemia

45
00:02:46,740 --> 00:02:52,380
in an effort to try to shunt the blood towards other areas of the lung that perhaps maybe are

46
00:02:52,380 --> 00:02:53,200
receiving oxygen.

47
00:02:53,560 --> 00:02:57,420
However, in patients with COPD, for example, or interstitial lung disease,

48
00:02:57,420 --> 00:03:01,200
there are no really good areas in the lung that are receiving oxygen.

49
00:03:01,600 --> 00:03:05,620
And hence, the entire lung is clamping down, resulting in secondary pulmonary hypertension.

50
00:03:06,400 --> 00:03:11,700
You also get secondary pulmonary hypertension with increased volume in the pulmonary circuit.

51
00:03:12,200 --> 00:03:16,400
Now, when you put extra blood into the vessels, the vessels will undergo smooth muscle hypertrophy,

52
00:03:16,600 --> 00:03:19,000
which will then result in pulmonary hypertension.

53
00:03:19,560 --> 00:03:22,100
And the classic case here would be congenital heart disease.

54
00:03:22,100 --> 00:03:27,320
And we've already discussed this in the cardiac chapter, in particular in the context of, for example,

55
00:03:27,760 --> 00:03:28,940
Eisenmenger's syndrome.

56
00:03:30,120 --> 00:03:35,240
A third way by which you can develop secondary pulmonary hypertension would be recurrent pulmonary embolism.

57
00:03:35,520 --> 00:03:36,500
This is very high yield.

58
00:03:36,600 --> 00:03:37,980
Let's go back and remind ourselves.

59
00:03:38,280 --> 00:03:41,220
When patients get PE, there's several things that can possibly occur.

60
00:03:41,680 --> 00:03:45,240
Most PE is asymptomatic, and that's because the lung has a dual circulation,

61
00:03:45,660 --> 00:03:48,160
and most pulmonary emboli are actually relatively small.

62
00:03:48,160 --> 00:03:52,260
So, again, the most common consequence of PE is that it's asymptomatic.

63
00:03:52,600 --> 00:03:54,580
Of course, you can also get pulmonary infarction.

64
00:03:54,940 --> 00:03:56,520
That would be a second consequence.

65
00:03:56,880 --> 00:03:58,720
A third consequence would be sudden death.

66
00:03:58,820 --> 00:04:00,760
That's classically seen with saddle emboli.

67
00:04:01,300 --> 00:04:07,540
And remember that the fourth consequence of PE is where you have chronic, long-standing PE that get reorganized,

68
00:04:07,800 --> 00:04:10,960
and that reorganization would result in thickening of the vascular wall,

69
00:04:11,220 --> 00:04:13,740
which could then result in pulmonary hypertension.

70
00:04:14,220 --> 00:04:16,780
And this is particularly high yield to be aware of as well.

71
00:04:16,780 --> 00:04:21,660
And so if I had to summarize this particular section in just a few very high yield points,

72
00:04:22,000 --> 00:04:25,580
I would say BMPR2 inactivating mutations, that's high yield,

73
00:04:25,900 --> 00:04:30,800
and plexiform lesions as a consequence of that, that's particularly high yield.

74
00:04:31,040 --> 00:04:34,120
And then don't forget that they get atherosclerosis of the pulmonary artery.

75
00:04:34,640 --> 00:04:38,440
And so that's a quick summary of what are the super high yields in this particular section.

76
00:04:38,440 --> 00:04:44,840
Moving on to respiratory distress syndromes, the first syndrome that we want to talk about is ARDS,

77
00:04:45,480 --> 00:04:47,240
or acute respiratory distress syndrome.

78
00:04:47,660 --> 00:04:53,240
The idea here is that you get damage to the alveolar-capillary interface within the air sacs.

79
00:04:53,700 --> 00:04:56,340
Now, if there's damage to the alveolar-capillary interface,

80
00:04:56,600 --> 00:05:00,780
this results in leaking a protein-rich fluid into the air sac.

81
00:05:00,780 --> 00:05:07,360
The protein-rich fluid is eventually then reorganized as something called a highline membrane.

82
00:05:08,100 --> 00:05:10,420
And that's the hallmark of this particular disorder,

83
00:05:10,540 --> 00:05:13,840
the formation of highline membranes diffusely throughout the lung.

84
00:05:14,280 --> 00:05:16,160
Here's an illustration of that.

85
00:05:16,240 --> 00:05:19,200
You can see that these are the air sacs of the lung that I'm circling.

86
00:05:19,680 --> 00:05:21,940
And you can see that each of the air sacs of the lung

87
00:05:21,940 --> 00:05:25,420
is clearly lined by this dense pink highline membrane.

88
00:05:25,420 --> 00:05:28,200
And, of course, that's the hallmark of ARDS.

89
00:05:29,240 --> 00:05:31,420
Now, with the formation of these highline membranes,

90
00:05:31,580 --> 00:05:33,620
two clinical problems are going to arise.

91
00:05:33,980 --> 00:05:38,240
First of all, you've thickened the diffusion barrier for the exchange of gases.

92
00:05:38,760 --> 00:05:42,660
And so the patients will not be able to exchange gas very well throughout the lung.

93
00:05:43,720 --> 00:05:46,720
And as a result, they develop hypoxemia and cyanosis.

94
00:05:47,280 --> 00:05:48,440
And that's the first problem.

95
00:05:48,760 --> 00:05:51,520
The second problem with the development of highline membranes

96
00:05:51,520 --> 00:05:54,080
is that these highline membranes are sticky

97
00:05:54,080 --> 00:05:57,560
and will increase the surface tension of the alveolar air sac.

98
00:05:57,780 --> 00:06:00,980
That's going to create a pressure on the alveolar air sac to collapse.

99
00:06:01,420 --> 00:06:03,620
And so you're going to get diffuse collapse of the lung

100
00:06:03,620 --> 00:06:06,240
as a consequence of the highline membranes as well.

101
00:06:06,440 --> 00:06:08,420
So not only can you not exchange gas,

102
00:06:08,620 --> 00:06:11,140
but you're not able to keep the air sacs open as well.

103
00:06:12,240 --> 00:06:13,720
And that's why I say here in your text

104
00:06:13,720 --> 00:06:17,820
that the clinical features are hypoxemia and cyanosis with respiratory distress.

105
00:06:18,700 --> 00:06:20,620
Now, were you to do a chest x-ray on these patients,

106
00:06:20,720 --> 00:06:22,760
you would see diffuse whiteout of the lung.

107
00:06:22,760 --> 00:06:25,720
And that's a classic finding in ARDS.

108
00:06:27,040 --> 00:06:29,220
There are multiple etiologies of ARDS.

109
00:06:29,760 --> 00:06:32,400
In fact, were you to look at a list of causes of ARDS,

110
00:06:32,580 --> 00:06:33,820
it would be in the hundreds.

111
00:06:34,300 --> 00:06:37,080
But just as an example, sepsis can do it,

112
00:06:37,140 --> 00:06:41,560
infection, shock, trauma, aspiration, pancreatitis, DIC, etc.

113
00:06:41,800 --> 00:06:44,120
So multiple things can result in ARDS.

114
00:06:44,780 --> 00:06:47,380
One of the underlying themes, regardless of the etiology,

115
00:06:47,540 --> 00:06:49,340
is that there's activation of the neutrophils

116
00:06:49,340 --> 00:06:52,940
with protease-mediated and free radical-mediated damage

117
00:06:52,940 --> 00:06:56,480
of type 1 and type 2 pneumocytes.

118
00:06:56,800 --> 00:06:57,720
And that's important to note,

119
00:06:57,800 --> 00:07:01,740
that both the type 1 and type 2 pneumocytes get damaged in this disorder.

120
00:07:02,300 --> 00:07:05,060
Now, the treatment involves addressing the underlying cause,

121
00:07:05,180 --> 00:07:09,240
because, of course, that's the reason that the patient has gone into ARDS.

122
00:07:09,240 --> 00:07:11,900
In addition, one of the key treatments for these patients

123
00:07:11,900 --> 00:07:15,760
is going to be ventilation with positive end expiratory pressure,

124
00:07:16,020 --> 00:07:17,020
also known as PEEP.

125
00:07:17,420 --> 00:07:21,780
Now, the idea here is that the patients have collapse of their air sacs

126
00:07:21,780 --> 00:07:25,440
due to the increased surface tension created by the highline membranes.

127
00:07:25,760 --> 00:07:28,440
And so every time they breathe out, the lung collapses.

128
00:07:28,840 --> 00:07:31,780
And then they have to place a tremendous stress on the lung

129
00:07:31,780 --> 00:07:33,780
to be able to blow it open again.

130
00:07:34,080 --> 00:07:36,400
And then again, when they breathe out, it collapses.

131
00:07:36,400 --> 00:07:38,640
And then, of course, it takes a tremendous amount of energy

132
00:07:38,640 --> 00:07:39,940
to try to blow it open again.

133
00:07:40,300 --> 00:07:42,940
And so one thing that can be done on the ventilator

134
00:07:42,940 --> 00:07:46,200
is that you can leave a little bit of pressure in the lung

135
00:07:46,200 --> 00:07:47,560
at the end of expiration

136
00:07:47,560 --> 00:07:50,840
so that those balloons stay open and don't collapse,

137
00:07:51,220 --> 00:07:54,920
which then allows for recruitment of additional alveolar air sacs,

138
00:07:54,920 --> 00:07:58,120
which then provides the patient with a chance to oxygenate that lung.

139
00:07:58,520 --> 00:07:59,940
Now, it's important also to note

140
00:07:59,940 --> 00:08:03,100
that recovery can be complicated by interstitial fibrosis.

141
00:08:03,480 --> 00:08:04,860
This is particularly high yield.

142
00:08:04,860 --> 00:08:08,540
And the reason you get interstitial fibrosis in the recovery

143
00:08:08,540 --> 00:08:10,960
is because you knocked out your type 2 pneumocytes.

144
00:08:11,080 --> 00:08:13,300
Now, remember that the type 2 pneumocytes are the stem cells.

145
00:08:13,740 --> 00:08:15,660
And so when you knock out the stem cells,

146
00:08:15,720 --> 00:08:17,380
you won't be able to regenerate the lung.

147
00:08:17,760 --> 00:08:19,680
And instead, were recovery to occur,

148
00:08:19,960 --> 00:08:21,660
it would be complicated by fibrosis

149
00:08:21,660 --> 00:08:25,260
because, again, you would get repair instead of regeneration.

150
00:08:26,560 --> 00:08:28,800
The next respiratory distress syndrome

151
00:08:28,800 --> 00:08:31,020
is called neonatal respiratory distress syndrome.

152
00:08:31,020 --> 00:08:33,580
This is respiratory distress that arises

153
00:08:33,580 --> 00:08:35,880
due to inadequate surfactant levels.

154
00:08:36,180 --> 00:08:37,680
Before you can understand this disorder,

155
00:08:37,800 --> 00:08:38,840
you've got to take a step back

156
00:08:38,840 --> 00:08:42,880
and just remind yourselves that surfactant is produced in the lung.

157
00:08:43,240 --> 00:08:45,240
It's produced by type 2 pneumocytes.

158
00:08:45,360 --> 00:08:46,740
That's particularly high yield.

159
00:08:47,120 --> 00:08:48,560
Remember that the type 2 pneumocytes,

160
00:08:48,700 --> 00:08:49,640
they have two functions.

161
00:08:49,820 --> 00:08:51,420
They are the stem cell of the lung,

162
00:08:51,540 --> 00:08:53,320
and they also produce surfactant.

163
00:08:53,680 --> 00:08:55,080
Now, the purpose of surfactant

164
00:08:55,080 --> 00:08:57,300
is to reduce the surface tension of the lung

165
00:08:57,300 --> 00:09:00,520
so that when you breathe out, the air sacs don't collapse.

166
00:09:00,880 --> 00:09:02,560
Let's just take a step back and remind ourselves.

167
00:09:02,660 --> 00:09:05,160
If I were to bring a 7-year-old child in the room

168
00:09:05,160 --> 00:09:07,540
and I were to ask that child to blow up a balloon,

169
00:09:08,100 --> 00:09:10,000
it would be hard for the child to do so.

170
00:09:10,300 --> 00:09:12,860
However, if I were to put a little bit of air into that balloon

171
00:09:12,860 --> 00:09:14,820
and then hand it to the 7-year-old child,

172
00:09:15,300 --> 00:09:17,120
they would be able to add air to the balloon.

173
00:09:17,560 --> 00:09:19,880
So the hardest part of expanding a balloon

174
00:09:19,880 --> 00:09:22,720
is that initial addition of air.

175
00:09:23,360 --> 00:09:26,640
The same exact principle applies to the alveolar air sacs.

176
00:09:26,640 --> 00:09:29,740
The greatest pressure required to blow open the sac

177
00:09:29,740 --> 00:09:33,020
is in that very initial addition of air.

178
00:09:33,520 --> 00:09:35,300
Now, of course, you don't want to have to spend

179
00:09:35,300 --> 00:09:36,560
a tremendous amount of energy

180
00:09:36,560 --> 00:09:38,940
blowing open the lung every time you blow out.

181
00:09:39,160 --> 00:09:41,420
If every time you breathed out, the lung collapsed,

182
00:09:41,480 --> 00:09:43,840
you'd have to spend a ton of energy blowing it open again.

183
00:09:44,240 --> 00:09:45,820
And so the lung produces surfactant,

184
00:09:45,920 --> 00:09:47,560
which decreases the surface tension,

185
00:09:48,180 --> 00:09:49,380
leaving the air sacs open

186
00:09:49,380 --> 00:09:50,820
so that the next time you breathe in,

187
00:09:50,940 --> 00:09:53,080
you don't have to use a tremendous amount of energy

188
00:09:53,080 --> 00:09:53,880
to breathe in.

189
00:09:53,880 --> 00:09:56,660
Now, of course, if a patient has a lack of surfactant,

190
00:09:56,980 --> 00:09:58,660
then every time they breathe in,

191
00:09:58,740 --> 00:10:00,180
they're going to be using a ton of energy,

192
00:10:00,540 --> 00:10:04,200
and they're very quickly going to develop respiratory failure.

193
00:10:04,540 --> 00:10:07,540
And that's the idea in neonatal respiratory distress syndrome.

194
00:10:08,260 --> 00:10:09,860
And so the key clinical features

195
00:10:09,860 --> 00:10:11,400
that the patients are going to present with

196
00:10:11,400 --> 00:10:14,040
are increasing respiratory effort after birth.

197
00:10:14,260 --> 00:10:16,060
And that's because every time they breathe in,

198
00:10:16,300 --> 00:10:18,080
they're using a tremendous amount of energy

199
00:10:18,080 --> 00:10:19,140
to blow open the lung.

200
00:10:19,140 --> 00:10:21,260
Now, eventually, that'll result in tachypnea,

201
00:10:21,620 --> 00:10:23,240
use of accessory muscles

202
00:10:23,240 --> 00:10:26,320
in order to continue to blow open the lung,

203
00:10:26,660 --> 00:10:27,240
and grunting.

204
00:10:27,720 --> 00:10:28,500
Eventually, of course,

205
00:10:28,580 --> 00:10:31,220
the patients will develop hypoxemia and cyanosis.

206
00:10:31,700 --> 00:10:33,600
And one of the key findings on chest x-ray

207
00:10:33,600 --> 00:10:35,960
will be diffuse granularity of the lung.

208
00:10:36,360 --> 00:10:38,200
Now, this is what the chest x-ray looks like.

209
00:10:38,440 --> 00:10:39,800
And the idea here is that

210
00:10:39,800 --> 00:10:41,100
if you were to look at the lung fields,

211
00:10:41,180 --> 00:10:44,360
you can see that there's sort of this hazy granularity

212
00:10:44,360 --> 00:10:47,540
or grainy appearance to the lungs.

213
00:10:47,740 --> 00:10:49,320
And that's a classic finding

214
00:10:49,320 --> 00:10:51,080
in patients who have

215
00:10:51,080 --> 00:10:53,360
neonatal respiratory distress syndrome.

216
00:10:53,920 --> 00:10:56,880
Now, the key causes of this disorder are three.

217
00:10:57,180 --> 00:11:00,200
Number one, and most obvious, would be prematurity.

218
00:11:00,640 --> 00:11:02,280
Remember that surfactant production

219
00:11:02,280 --> 00:11:04,500
begins at about 28 weeks gestation,

220
00:11:04,960 --> 00:11:07,640
and adequate levels are reached at about 34 weeks.

221
00:11:07,980 --> 00:11:09,320
Now, if we have a preemie

222
00:11:09,320 --> 00:11:11,540
who hasn't reached adequate levels,

223
00:11:11,540 --> 00:11:13,280
of course, they're going to develop

224
00:11:13,280 --> 00:11:15,520
neonatal respiratory distress syndrome.

225
00:11:16,060 --> 00:11:17,680
Now, it's also very high yield to know

226
00:11:17,680 --> 00:11:20,640
that you can screen for adequacy of surfactant

227
00:11:20,640 --> 00:11:22,720
using something called the LS ratio.

228
00:11:23,240 --> 00:11:24,960
The L stands for lecithin,

229
00:11:25,320 --> 00:11:27,240
and the S stands for sphingomyelin.

230
00:11:27,740 --> 00:11:29,140
And what you need to know here is that

231
00:11:29,140 --> 00:11:32,820
as the lung matures from about 28 weeks to 34 weeks

232
00:11:32,820 --> 00:11:34,400
as surfactant is being produced,

233
00:11:34,780 --> 00:11:37,160
the key component in surfactant is lecithin.

234
00:11:37,540 --> 00:11:39,440
And so lecithin will increase

235
00:11:39,440 --> 00:11:42,700
from 28 weeks to 34 weeks, for example,

236
00:11:43,060 --> 00:11:45,200
whereas sphingomyelin will stay constant.

237
00:11:45,620 --> 00:11:48,280
And so as the lung increases production

238
00:11:48,280 --> 00:11:50,960
of the lecithin component of surfactant,

239
00:11:51,240 --> 00:11:52,280
that's going to then increase

240
00:11:52,280 --> 00:11:54,580
the lecithin to sphingomyelin ratio.

241
00:11:55,100 --> 00:11:56,720
And when the ratio is greater than two,

242
00:11:57,080 --> 00:11:59,180
that indicates that the lung has now reached

243
00:11:59,180 --> 00:12:01,700
adequate levels of surfactant production.

244
00:12:02,160 --> 00:12:03,500
Very high yield to also know

245
00:12:03,500 --> 00:12:05,680
that the major component in surfactant

246
00:12:05,680 --> 00:12:07,440
is called phosphatidylcholine.

247
00:12:07,440 --> 00:12:09,300
And by the way, phosphatidylcholine

248
00:12:09,300 --> 00:12:10,540
is actually lecithin.

249
00:12:10,900 --> 00:12:12,860
So sometimes the examiners will ask you,

250
00:12:13,040 --> 00:12:14,900
what's the major component of surfactant?

251
00:12:15,800 --> 00:12:17,620
Phosphatidylcholine, which again is lecithin,

252
00:12:17,860 --> 00:12:20,060
which will increase as the lung matures.

253
00:12:20,560 --> 00:12:23,460
And when the ratio of lecithin or phosphatidylcholine

254
00:12:23,460 --> 00:12:25,200
to sphingomyelin is greater than two,

255
00:12:25,580 --> 00:12:27,920
that indicates adequate lung maturity.

256
00:12:28,340 --> 00:12:31,060
Another important risk factor is C-section delivery.

257
00:12:31,420 --> 00:12:32,980
The idea here is that steroids,

258
00:12:33,200 --> 00:12:34,140
they do a few things.

259
00:12:34,240 --> 00:12:37,040
Number one, they increase the production of surfactant,

260
00:12:37,040 --> 00:12:39,520
and number two, they cause surfactant

261
00:12:39,520 --> 00:12:41,780
that is present within the type 2 pneumocytes

262
00:12:41,780 --> 00:12:43,100
to be released into the lung.

263
00:12:43,580 --> 00:12:45,360
And so that applies to this discussion

264
00:12:45,360 --> 00:12:46,620
because when the baby is going

265
00:12:46,620 --> 00:12:47,680
through the birthing process,

266
00:12:48,020 --> 00:12:49,460
that's a very stressful period

267
00:12:49,460 --> 00:12:51,920
to be contracted on by the uterine wall

268
00:12:51,920 --> 00:12:53,520
and then to be pushed through the birth canal

269
00:12:53,520 --> 00:12:54,820
creates a tremendous stress.

270
00:12:55,300 --> 00:12:57,560
Of course, that stress results in release of steroids.

271
00:12:57,920 --> 00:12:59,600
If a baby undergoes C-section delivery,

272
00:12:59,740 --> 00:13:00,600
there is no stress,

273
00:13:00,880 --> 00:13:02,320
and so steroids won't be released.

274
00:13:02,740 --> 00:13:04,300
And so there's going to be an increased risk

275
00:13:04,300 --> 00:13:06,620
of neonatal respiratory distress syndrome.

276
00:13:06,960 --> 00:13:09,000
The final association is maternal diabetes.

277
00:13:09,360 --> 00:13:11,440
The idea here is that if a woman has diabetes,

278
00:13:11,540 --> 00:13:12,620
she's got a high blood sugar,

279
00:13:12,940 --> 00:13:15,060
that high blood sugar is going to go into the baby.

280
00:13:15,360 --> 00:13:17,380
And of course, the baby has a normal pancreas.

281
00:13:17,740 --> 00:13:19,460
The baby's pancreas will respond

282
00:13:19,460 --> 00:13:21,120
by producing excess insulin,

283
00:13:21,120 --> 00:13:24,780
and insulin is an inhibitor of surfactant production.

284
00:13:25,180 --> 00:13:27,240
So because the baby has high levels of insulin,

285
00:13:27,640 --> 00:13:30,020
that's going to result in an increased risk

286
00:13:30,020 --> 00:13:32,400
for neonatal respiratory distress syndrome.

287
00:13:32,920 --> 00:13:35,020
Now, there are a few important complications to note

288
00:13:35,020 --> 00:13:37,200
concerning neonatal respiratory distress syndrome.

289
00:13:37,560 --> 00:13:40,500
The first is that hypoxemia present in the child

290
00:13:40,500 --> 00:13:44,000
will increase the risk for persistence of the PDA.

291
00:13:44,300 --> 00:13:45,720
Remember that the PDA closes

292
00:13:45,720 --> 00:13:48,180
when there's good oxygenation of the blood.

293
00:13:48,900 --> 00:13:51,140
And the second is that the patient would be at risk

294
00:13:51,140 --> 00:13:53,340
for developing a necrotizing enterocolitis

295
00:13:53,340 --> 00:13:55,980
due to the fact that there's decreased oxygen

296
00:13:55,980 --> 00:13:57,400
going to the gut.

297
00:13:57,400 --> 00:13:59,580
And the final complication is that

298
00:13:59,580 --> 00:14:01,660
when patients are given supplemental oxygen,

299
00:14:01,880 --> 00:14:04,300
as is classically used in treatment of this disorder,

300
00:14:04,600 --> 00:14:07,260
that's going to result in increased production of free radicals.

301
00:14:07,600 --> 00:14:09,960
Now, those free radicals can injure two major tissues.

302
00:14:10,300 --> 00:14:12,380
The first is that the free radicals get in the blood,

303
00:14:12,440 --> 00:14:13,680
and they can injure the retina,

304
00:14:13,960 --> 00:14:15,180
which would then lead to blindness.

305
00:14:16,160 --> 00:14:19,660
And the use of oxygen in neonatal respiratory distress syndrome

306
00:14:19,660 --> 00:14:23,260
used to be one of the major causes of blindness in neonates.

307
00:14:23,700 --> 00:14:26,180
In addition, remember that when a baby is born,

308
00:14:26,180 --> 00:14:27,660
the lung is relatively immature,

309
00:14:28,020 --> 00:14:29,780
and it takes a couple more years

310
00:14:29,780 --> 00:14:31,140
for the lung to completely develop.

311
00:14:31,520 --> 00:14:33,200
Now, if that immature lung is hit

312
00:14:33,200 --> 00:14:34,740
with a tremendous amount of oxygen,

313
00:14:34,960 --> 00:14:38,580
that would then result in excessive generation of free radicals,

314
00:14:38,640 --> 00:14:40,700
and those free radicals would damage the lung

315
00:14:40,700 --> 00:14:42,680
so that over time it wouldn't form properly.

316
00:14:43,120 --> 00:14:45,400
And that's called bronchopulmonary dysplasia.

317
00:14:46,000 --> 00:14:48,140
And so these are some complications to be aware of

318
00:14:48,140 --> 00:14:50,400
concerning neonatal respiratory distress syndrome.

319
00:14:50,400 --> 00:15:19,840
Thank you.

